PRIVATE REFLECTION: The five positives dad dab gut low cat are all C V C and are e,m,p,r-free; the ten letters they use are {a,b,c,d,g,l,o,t,u,w}. The five quoted negatives ear cop ego mom ate all contain at least one of e,m,p,r and/or break the C V C shape. Hence the separating description that is repeatedly invoked is “C V C and e,m,p,r-free”, equivalently a C V C word built from the a b c d g l o t u w alphabet, with in the exemplars middle ∈ {a,u,o} and final ∈ {d,b,t,w}.

On that description the options are:
* leg = l e g contains e, so e,m,p,r-violating;
* dam = d a m contains m and ends in m ∉ {d,b,t,w};
* rat = r a t contains r, first consonant r is outside the exemplar first set and r is forbidden;
* car = c a r contains r and ends in r ∉ {d,b,t,w};
* bin = b i n is C V C and contains no e,m,p,r.

So A–D are all non-members of the dad/dab/gut/low/cat family and E is the only option that is e,m,p,r-free and C V C. The majority of colleagues therefore treat E. bin as the unique pattern-following item and A–D, e.g. A. leg, as canonical non-following examples. 

Note of tension: bin introduces the new vowel i and the new final consonant n, which are not present in the five positives; colleague 9 stresses the exemplar middle {a,u,o} / final {d,b,t,w} restriction, under which bin also fails and all five options fail the strict exemplar-shape, with rat being the closest on middle a / final t. This i/n novelty is unresolved, but the e,m,p,r-free C V C reading is the dominant consensus so far.

